# Requirements

Oriel apps are built with **Zig 0.16** and run on the system webview:

- **Zig 0.16.0**: optional. The `oriel` CLI uses `zig` on PATH when it is the project's version, and otherwise installs it into `~/.oriel/zig` on first use (downloaded from a community mirror and minisign-verified; see `oriel zig --help`).
- **Node.js and npm** for the React, Vue and Svelte templates (Vite). Installed automatically without admin rights via `oriel setup node` (or `oriel doctor --fix`). The `vanilla` template needs no Node at all.
- **Linux:** GTK 4 and WebKitGTK 6.0 development packages (`gtk4` and `webkitgtk-6.0` on Arch; `libgtk-4-dev` and `libwebkitgtk-6.0-dev` on Debian and Ubuntu; `gtk4-devel` and `webkitgtk6.0-devel` on Fedora).
- **Windows:** the WebView2 runtime (built into Windows 11 and current Windows 10), `WebView2Loader.dll` (cached via `oriel webview2`), and NSIS for installers. You can install tools without administrator rights using `oriel setup node`, `oriel setup nsis`, and `oriel webview2` (or automatically via `oriel doctor --fix`), or via winget: `winget install --id OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS` and `winget install --id NSIS.NSIS` (note: NSIS via winget requires administrator privileges / UAC elevation). See [Building & packaging]($link.page('docs/building')).
- **macOS:** Xcode or the Xcode command-line tools (`xcode-select --install`).

`oriel doctor --fix` checks all of this and installs what it can without administrator rights.

# Install the CLI

The `oriel` CLI is a single static binary. It scaffolds apps and wraps their build steps, like `create-tauri-app` and `tauri dev` / `tauri build`.

On **Linux and macOS**:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/highercomve/Oriel/main/install.sh | sh
```

It installs to `~/.local/bin/oriel`, verifies the download against the release's `SHA256SUMS`, and never uses sudo.

On **Windows** (PowerShell):

```powershell
irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/highercomve/Oriel/main/install.ps1 | iex
```

If PowerShell script execution is blocked on your system, run:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process Bypass
```

before running `irm ... | iex`.

It installs per user to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\oriel` with no administrator rights, also verified against `SHA256SUMS`.

Both installers read the same environment variables:

| Variable | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `ORIEL_VERSION` | newest release | Tag to install, e.g. `v0.3.0` |
| `ORIEL_INSTALL_DIR` | `~/.local/bin` or `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\oriel` | Where to put the binary |

## First-run workflow

The standard first-run workflow on a clean machine:

1. **Install the CLI** using the commands above.
2. **Run `oriel doctor --fix`** to verify your environment, install non-admin dependencies automatically (Zig, Node.js LTS, WebView2Loader.dll, and portable NSIS on Windows), and print commands for any missing system packages.
3. **Create an app** with `oriel init <name>`.

# Updating the CLI

The CLI can update itself in place using Oriel's built-in self-updater:

```sh
oriel update --check          # check if an update is available without installing
oriel update                  # download, verify, and replace the running binary
oriel update --version v0.3.0 # target a specific release tag
```

Release manifests are Ed25519-signed and verified against the public key embedded in the binary. For non-interactive scripts and CI environments, pass `--yes` to proceed without a confirmation prompt.

# Create an app

```sh
oriel init my-app
cd my-app
```

`init` creates `./my-app` with a React + Vite frontend, fetches the Zig dependencies and runs `npm install`. The options:

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--template <react\|vue\|svelte\|vanilla>` | Frontend template (default: `react`) |
| `--id <app-id>` | Application id in reverse-DNS form (default: `com.example.<Name>`) |
| `--oriel-ref <ref>` | Oriel tag or commit to depend on |
| `--oriel-path <dir>` | Depend on a local Oriel checkout instead |
| `--no-install` | Only write the files: skip `zig build --fetch` and `npm install` |
| `--no-webview2` | Skip downloading `WebView2Loader.dll` for Windows builds |

The new project looks like this:

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `src/main.zig` | The Zig side: `Commands` the page can call and `Events` pushed to it |
| `frontend/` | The page (Vite project, or a static page for `vanilla`) |
| `build.zig` | Which Oriel modules are compiled in, packaging metadata |
| `build.zig.zon` | Package manifest; Oriel is a dependency |
| `icon.png` | Application icon (1024×1024 PNG recommended) |

# Run it

```sh
oriel dev
```

`dev` starts the Vite dev server and opens the app against it, with hot reload for the frontend. Zig changes rebuild and restart the app. Dev builds use the app id with a `.Dev` suffix, so they run next to an installed copy.

On Windows, the first `oriel dev` with a Node.js installed by Oriel may show a Windows Firewall prompt for Node.js. The dev server only listens on localhost, so you can cancel it.

To build the real thing, with the frontend embedded in the executable:

```sh
oriel build            # zig-out/bin/my-app (ReleaseSafe by default)
oriel run              # build and run
```

Arguments after `--` go to the app, not to the build: `oriel run -- --verbose`.

# App icon

Apps configure their icon with a single source PNG (1024×1024 recommended) in `build.zig`:

```zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    .name = "my-app",
    .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
    .icon = b.path("icon.png"), // optional, defaults to Oriel brand icon
    ...
});
```

Projects created with `oriel init` include a default `icon.png`. At build time, Oriel automatically generates:
- **Windows**: Multi-resolution `.ico` embedded as an executable resource (`RT_GROUP_ICON`) and used for window icons, taskbar, shortcuts, and the installer.
- **Linux**: Hicolor theme icons and `.desktop` entry.
- **macOS**: `.icns` for the `.app` bundle and dynamic Dock icon.

# The other commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `oriel types` | Regenerate the frontend's TypeScript types for the Zig commands |
| `oriel check` | Type-check the app's Zig code without building (about a second) |
| `oriel package` | Build packages into `zig-out/package` (deb, rpm, AppImage on Linux; setup.exe on Windows; .app and .dmg on macOS) |
| `oriel zig` | Manage the Zig versions the CLI uses (`install`, `uninstall`, `list`, `which`) in `~/.oriel/zig` |
| `oriel webview2` | Download and cache Microsoft Edge `WebView2Loader.dll` for Windows |

Every command is a thin wrapper around `zig build <step>`, run from anywhere inside the project, and extra arguments are passed on: `oriel build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast` works, and so does plain `zig build`.

> Using Oriel without the CLI? Add it with the Zig package manager, `zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel`, and call `oriel.addApp()` from your `build.zig`. The generated project shows the exact wiring.

